# Docs: Windows / conda terminal shell examples

HABIT’s primary teaching shell on Windows is **Anaconda Prompt** / **Anaconda
PowerShell Prompt** (and similar conda terminals). Many users copy-paste from
the docs into that shell.

## No backslash line continuations

Do **not** write CLI examples like::

   habit view demo_data/.../image.nrrd \
              demo_data/.../subj001_habitats.nrrd

A trailing `\` newline is a Unix / cmd quirk. In conda PowerShell (and often
when users paste into Anaconda Prompt), it **breaks** or is treated as a
literal argument.

**Always put one shell command on one physical line** in rst `::` / code
blocks, even if the line is long::

   habit view demo_data/preprocessed/images/subj001/LAP/WATER__WATER__Ax_Dyn_LAVA_Flex+C_Series0009.nrrd demo_data/results/habitat_two_step/subj001_habitats.nrrd

If readability suffers, shorten paths with placeholders on the **same** line,
or split into two separate one-line commands — never `\` continuation.

## Related Windows shell constraints (same audience)

- Prefer `cd D:\my_habit_work` — do **not** use `cd /d` (fails in conda
  PowerShell).
- Process-pool Python snippets need `if __name__ == "__main__":` (see
  `docs/source/_includes/windows_multiprocessing.rst`).

## When editing docs

Before finishing a docs change that adds or touches CLI examples, search for
trailing `\` line breaks in `docs/source/**/*.rst` and remove them.
